r reading as nothing changed; the audit list of every act-ledger entry naming a verdict, newest first, or None recorded; the pending owner actions; and, last, the Decisions to review section. It owns no second opinion: the queue is pending.open_item_queue, the attestation model trace.reattest_model, the pointers pending.pending_block and the decisions pending.decisions_to_review, imported rather than re-derived, so where this view and the trace.py --approve brief disagree, this view is the defect. NOT claimed: that the rendered page is legible or well-composed.\"\"\"","+detail = \"\"\"The rendered half of the attestation regime under SR-049. The page renders, in this order: the pending open items that queued or claimed work items cite in their needs, as decision briefs beside the queued or claimed rows citing them, preceded by an integrity notice naming and linking the pending items that no queued or claimed row cites, the page claiming an empty queue only when there is neither; every SR whose chain holds a row owing a first approval or drifted from the approved snapshot, with the chain's per-cell before/after, a section with no changed cell stating that no cell differs from the approved snapshot and never reading as nothing changed; the audit list of every act-ledger entry naming a verdict, newest first, or None recorded; the pending owner actions; and, last, the Decisions to review section. It owns no second opinion: the queue is pending.open_item_queue, the attestation model trace.reattest_model, the pointers pending.pending_block and the decisions pending.decisions_to_review, imported rather than re-derived, so where this view and the trace.py --approve brief disagree, this view is the defect. NOT claimed: that the rendered page is legible or well-composed.\"\"\""," rationale = \"\"\"A human cannot bless a delta they cannot see, and the depth that makes a re-attest readable is a WORD-LEVEL diff — which the markdown surface this replaces could not mark, so a reviewer was asked to sign a change they could only read as a whole cell. Re-deriving the attestation model here was rejected: a second opinion that disagreed with the brief would leave the reviewer arbitrating between two surfaces. Both emitted contracts exist because their absence fails SILENTLY in front of someone about to attest, which is the worst place for a quiet failure.\"\"\""," test_refs = \"(see TC-123)\""," status = \"Approved\""]},{"oldStart":1535,"oldLines":9,"newStart":1535,"newLines":9,"lines":[" [design.LLR-153]"," sr_refs = [\"SR-174\", \"SR-228\"]"," title = \"The unified trunk-side intake mint + the context block + the approval-level arms\"","-module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py\"","-code_symbol = \"\"\"intake_after_merge/_amendment_drafts/mint_gap_rows/parse_dispositions/parse_disposition_section/next_wi_id/context_block/_inject_open_item/_pending_oi_lines/adjudication_action/flip_verified/_standing_successors/_title_identity_refusal/_repoint_owed\"\"\"","-detail = \"\"\"The mint invariant: a WI id is created only by a human trunk commit or this helper - lanes never mint. next_wi_id counts from max(the docs/id-watermark mark, the sweep of every spec FILENAME under docs/work/ and docs/archive/work/, active/<branch>/ included) + 1 - never max(live) alone, so an id freed by a deleted spec is never re-issued - and trace.read_watermark's refusal on an absent or malformed mark is deliberately NOT caught: a mint with no record of what has been allocated must not proceed on a guess (TC-158, IF-101). Three triggers, serial by construction: (a) the approved/routed-cell diff on the merged commit via check_trajectory.staged_spine_amendments, whose walk covers the SR, LLR, TC, SN, DA and SUR ti
